{"@context": {"@vocab": "https://schema.org/", "dct": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/"}, "@type": "BioChemEntity", "@id": "https://cnvpathwayatlas.github.io/cnv-website/cnv/2q21-1/", "dct:conformsTo": {"@id": "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/BioChemEntity/0.7-RELEASE-2019_06_19"}, "additionalType": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0001019", "identifier": "2q21.1", "name": "2q21.1 Copy Number Variation", "url": "https://cnvpathwayatlas.github.io/cnv-website/cnv/2q21-1/", "isPartOf": {"@id": "https://cnvpathwayatlas.github.io/cnv-website/"}, "additionalProperty": [{"@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Cytogenetic location", "value": "2q21.1"}, {"@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Region GRCh37", "value": "Chr2:131481308-131930677"}, {"@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Region GRCh38", "value": "Chr2:130723735-131173104"}], "description": "The 2q21.1 copy number variation syndrome can result in the loss of up to 9 protein-coding genes. Deletions and duplications in 2q21.1 were reported to be connected to intellectual disability, hyperactivity, and aggressive behavior (Almuzzaini et al., 2020; Gimelli et al., 2014). The clinical picture was explained by alterations in five genes important for neurological development, namely GPR148, FAM123C, ARHGEF4, FAM168B and PLEKHB2 (Gimelli et al., 2014; Dharmadhikari et al., 2012). Analogically, changes in tubulin genes in 2q21.1 were linked to Motor Timing in ADHD (Rommelse et al., 2008). For this rare disorder, two different genomic locations are known according to Kirov et al. 2014, and Gimelli et al. 2014 with a larger deletion.", "hasBioChemEntityPart": [{"@type": "Gene", "dct:conformsTo": {"@id": "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Gene/1.0-RELEASE"}, "name": "GPR148", "@id": "https://identifiers.org/hgnc:23623", "identifier": "HGNC:23623", "url": "https://www.genenames.org/data/gene-symbol-report/#!/hgnc_id/HGNC:23623", "description": "G protein-coupled receptor 148", "sameAs": ["https://identifiers.org/ncbigene:344561", "https://ide